    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1098?c=unco": {
      "title": "Dean of the College of Performing and Visual Arts",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Dean of the College of Performing and Visual Arts Greeley, CO, United States | req1098 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/30/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Dean of the College of Performing and Visual Arts COMPENSATION RANGE: 170,000.00 - 195,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Perf & Vis Arts Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 7/1/2027 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on October 30, 2026 and must include a cover letter, resume/cv and 5 references. Position Summary: The Dean of the College of Performing & Visual Arts is the chief academic and administrative leader of the College which includes the Schools of Art and Design, Music, and Theatre Arts and Dance. The Dean is a key member of the University’s academic leadership team and is responsible for advancing the academic quality, creative excellence, student success, financial sustainability, and external impact of the College. This is a pivotal leadership role at a consequential moment for the University of Northern Colorado (UNC). As UNC considers how it will distinguish itself and serve Colorado and the region in the future, the performing and visual arts represent an area of significant institutional strength, visibility, and opportunity. The Dean will not only lead the College but will shape what the arts can become and how they contribute to the University’s evolving identity, educational mission, and public impact. The Dean will provide strategic and entrepreneurial leadership; align programs and resources with areas of strength and opportunity; cultivate a culture of academic excellence, collaboration, and shared governance; and expand the College’s reach through partnerships, philanthropy, community engagement, and innovative educational and artistic opportunities. Job Duties: Strategic Leadership & College Transformation – 20% Lead the College in defining and advancing its distinctive academic and artistic identity and its role in UNC’s future. Develop and execute a forward-looking strategy that identifies areas for investment, growth, redesign, partnership, and, when appropriate, consolidation or discontinuation. Advocate for the unique academic, artistic, production, facilities, and accreditation needs of the disciplines of the College. Foster innovation across disciplines and encourage new models of education, creative practice, performance, exhibition, and engagement. Work collaboratively with School Directors, faculty, staff, students, other deans, and University leadership to translate institutional priorities into a focused strategy for the College and to position the College as a distinctive area of strength for UNC. Academic Portfolio, Student Success & Enrollment – 20% Ensure academic quality and appropriate accreditation while continually evaluating the College’s academic portfolio in relation to student demand, enrollment, student outcomes, professional and workforce opportunities, institutional mission, cost, and capacity. Lead strategies to recruit, retain, and graduate students in collaboration with College enrollment staff, School Directors, faculty, and central enrollment offices. Support curricular innovation and develop educational opportunities responsive to emerging learners, professions, and markets while maintaining the artistic and scholarly standards of the disciplines. Coordinate the mission and goals of the University, College, and Schools with the requirements of pertinent professional accrediting bodies (NAST, NASM) and the Higher Learning Commission. Resource Stewardship & Organizational Effectiveness—15% Strategically allocate faculty, staff, budget, space, facilities, and other resources to advance College and University priorities. Use evidence and data to inform decisions regarding academic capacity, faculty workload, staffing, program sustainability, and investment. Develop organizational structures and operating practices that enable the College to use its resources effectively and sustainably. Represent College resource needs to central administration and oversee budget development, equipment, technology, and other major resource requests. Faculty, Staff & Organizational Leadership—15% Recruit, develop, evaluate, and retain excellent faculty and staff; cultivate strong academic leadership within the College; and support faculty achievement, scholarship, creative activity, teaching, and professional development. Carry out personnel responsibilities, including annual and comprehensive evaluation, promotion and tenure, workload oversight, and disciplinary actions, in accordance with university policy. Build a culture capable of navigating change while maintaining academic excellence and meaningful shared governance. External Engagement, Partnerships & Advancement – 20% Position the College as a major cultural and creative presence for UNC, northern Colorado, and the region. Build and sustain relationships with alumni, donors, patrons, schools, arts organizations, community partners, businesses, foundations, and other external constituencies. Partner with University Advancement and other offices to expand philanthropic support, grants, sponsorships, earned revenue, and other external resources. Expand the reach and impact of the College through performances, exhibitions, community programming, professional and continuing education, partnerships, and other opportunities that connect UNC’s artistic and creative expertise with learners and audiences beyond traditional degree programs. There is an expectation that the Dean will attend concerts, theatrical productions, and gallery events, many of which take place on evenings and weekends. University Leadership & Collaboration – 10% Serve as an institutional leader whose responsibilities extend beyond the College. Collaborate with other deans and University leaders to advance UNC’s broader academic strategy and institutional effectiveness, including opportunities that connect the arts with education, health and well-being, research and creative activity, community engagement, and emerging interdisciplinary areas. Participate actively in University planning, shared governance, and leadership initiatives. Minimum Qualifications: A completed terminal degree in a field represented within, or closely related to, the disciplines of the college (i.e., Arts, Dance, Music, Theatre). A record of teaching, scholarship, creative/professional achievement, and service sufficient to qualify for appointment at the rank of Professor at UNC (at least seven years of successful academic performance and substantial evidence documenting outstanding professional performance). Progressively responsible academic leadership experience appropriate to the scope of a college dean, such as service as dean, associate or assistant dean, school director, department chair, or comparable leadership role. Evidence of leadership in several of the following areas: organizational change; academic portfolio development and review; enrollment strategy and student success; financial and resource stewardship; external partnerships; philanthropy or advancement; interdisciplinary collaboration; and development of new or innovative educational, artistic, or revenue-generating opportunities. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated experience leading within a shared governance, complex higher education budget environment. Demonstrated experience with effective enrollment management. Demonstrated experience managing change, innovation, and making difficult decisions in a collaborative and inclusive manner. Demonstrated experience collaborating with stakeholders and colleagues across a broad range of groups, backgrounds, and positions. Demonstrated experience in working closely with faculty within and across a college in developing innovative academic programs. Ability to interpret qualitative and quantitative data and the ability to use these data in a collaborative decision-making process. Ability to solve problems creatively while working to build consensus that effectively moves the college forward. A high degree of creativity and mature intellectual judgment, as well as a high level of empathy and diplomacy in working with stakeholders within the institution and in the community. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1094?c=unco": {
      "title": "Tech Pro - Assistant Technical Director - Campus Commons",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Tech Pro - Assistant Technical Director - Campus Commons Greeley, CO, United States | req1094 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/25/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Tech Pro - Assistant Technical Director - Campus Commons COMPENSATION RANGE: 4,000.00 - 36,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Temporary DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Perf & Vis Arts Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 10/1/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on October 3, 2026. Position Summary: This is a temporary position is expected to last between 1 to 9 months, with a compensation of $4,000 per month. The Campus Commons Assistant Technical Director supports the safe, efficient, and professional operation of the Campus Commons Performance Hall and other PVA performance and event spaces. The position provides hands-on technical production support in lighting, audio, video, rigging, and stage operations; supervises and trains student production staff; and serves as a technical lead for rehearsals, performances, and special events. Working as part of the PVA Production Team, the Assistant Technical Director collaborates with faculty, staff, students, guest artists, and external clients to translate production requirements into safe and practical technical solutions. The position also assists with maintenance, inspection, organization, and continuous improvement of performance spaces, theatrical systems, equipment, and production procedures. Job Duties: 50% Technical Production & Event Operations Serve as technical lead or production technician for rehearsals, performances, concerts, rentals, and special events. Operate, program, install, configure, and troubleshoot theatrical lighting, audio, video, projection, communications, and related production systems. Safely operate theatrical rigging systems, personnel lifts, stage machinery, acoustic shells, and other specialized equipment. Coordinate technical setup, changeover, strike, and restoration of performance spaces. Interpret event requirements and determine safe, efficient methods for accomplishing production needs. Troubleshoot technical problems during live events and make timely operational decisions. 20% Supervision of Student Work Crew Direct, supervise, and coordinate student production crews during calls and events. Assign work, communicate expectations, monitor performance, and provide coaching and corrective feedback. Train student employees in safe operation of production equipment and established procedures. Model professional production practices, customer service, teamwork, and workplace safety. Assist the Technical Director and Production Team in developing and maintaining training materials, SOPs, and safety procedures. 20% Venue & Equipment Maintenance Perform routine inspection, maintenance, troubleshooting, cleaning, and organization of performance spaces and production equipment. Identify equipment deficiencies and safety concerns and coordinate repair or replacement with the Technical Director. Maintain organized backstage, storage, booth, shop, and technical work areas. Assist with equipment inventory, preventative maintenance, documentation, and lifecycle planning. Support improvement projects involving theatrical infrastructure and production systems. 10% Other duties as Assisned The position frequently works independently during rehearsals, performances, and events and must make immediate decisions involving safety, equipment, personnel, scheduling, and production priorities. Many situations occur during live events where consultation with a supervisor is impractical and delays could result in injury, equipment damage, disruption of a performance, or a poor experience for artists and audiences. The Assistant Technical Director must evaluate technical and artistic requests, identify potential hazards or operational conflicts, and develop practical solutions within established safety standards, available resources, schedules, and production capabilities. Examples include diagnosing and resolving an audio, lighting, video, or control-system failure during a live performance; modifying a production plan when requested staging or equipment cannot be safely accommodated; or directing student crews through complex event changeovers under limited time constraints. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in theatre, technical production, entertainment technology, music technology, or a related field and two years of relevant professional experience; or an equivalent combination of education and professional experience. Demonstrated experience in live event or theatrical production. Working knowledge of at least two production disciplines such as audio, lighting, video/projection, rigging, or stage operations. Experience troubleshooting technical systems in a live production environment. Experience directing or coordinating the work of crews. Ability to safely work at height and operate specialized theatrical equipment following training. Strong organizational, communication, and customer-service skills. Ability to work independently, prioritize competing demands, and make sound decisions under time constraints. Ability and willingness to learn unfamiliar production systems and technologies Preferred experience: Experience with networked audio, lighting, or video systems, including Dante and DMX/sACN/Art-Net. Experience with theatrical rigging and counterweight systems. Experience with digital audio consoles and theatrical lighting control systems. Experience supervising or training student employees. Experience in higher education, performing arts centers, presenting organizations, or similar multi-use venues. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1093?c=unco": {
      "title": "Assoc Dir of Development",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Assoc Dir of Development Greeley, CO, United States | req1093 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/18/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Assoc Dir of Development COMPENSATION RANGE: 70,000.00 - 75,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: VP Alumni and Development Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 11/2/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 10/03/2026. Position Summary: The Associate Director of Development is responsible for identifying, cultivating, soliciting and stewarding leadership annual giving (LAG) donors from identified prospects within a portfolio comprised of university-wide donors, alumni, parents, faculty, staff and friends. This position primarily works with a portfolio of 150-200 individuals capable of making leadership gifts ($1,000-$24,999) to the university with a goal of increasing annual giving and developing a strong pipeline for major gift donors. This position will work collaboratively with development officers on the referral of major gift prospects and the Donor Relations Coordinator to coordiate stewardship efforts. They carry out these responsibilities on behalf of university-wide program(s), including academic and non-academic initiatives, and overall university priorities. This position reports to the Executive Director of Gift Planning and is a member of the centralized Development team within the division of University Advancement. This individual must foster inclusivity while working closely and collaboratively with all development, advancement services, annual giving, alumni and foundation staff members to ensure that philanthropic relationships, communications, events and solicitations are donor centric in order to maximize support of UNC. This individual also works closely and coordinates with university-wide administration, marketing and communications teams, and faculty and support staff to ensure that they are appropriately informed and engaged in development activities. Job Duties: Proactively manages a portfolio of 150-200 identified leadership annual giving (LAG) donors and prospects ($1,000-$24,999). Time spent: 65% Meets fundraising performance metrics established with supervisor on an annual basis. Metrics include but are not limited to substantive visits, qualification visits, total dollars solicited, retention rates and other strategic activities. Develops communication strategies to contact, via telephone, email and other methods, identified prospects to secure 1:1, face-to-face visits. Conducts personal visits with prospects to solicit, upgrade and retain LAG level prospects utilizing a variety of gift vehicles including multi-year pledges to assist the team in realizing their annual and overall Development goals. Makes assessments of prospects for the purpose of assessing capacity and inclination to make a major, planned, or blended gift to develop a strong pipeline for major gift prospects. Once identified, coordinates with development leadership and development officers on the referral of major gift level ($25,000 or more) prospects. Creates effective, actionable cultivation and solicitation plans. Occasionally prepares compelling written proposals to secure gifts that address the donor’s philanthropic interests and university and/or unit priorities. Portfolio Management and Stewardship. Time spent: 20% Documents completed and anticipated moves with prospects in alumni/development database in a timely and accurate way. Routinely collaborates with Donor Relations Coordinator to ensure appropriate stewardship process is in place and executed for all donors within assigned portfolio and, in collaboration with development officers, implement strategy for engagement and program/college-specific stewardship. Works effectively and collaboratively with appropriate divisional colleagues on the execution of gift agreements, creation of endowed/spendable funds and receipting. Works closely with the prospect researcher to ensure that assigned prospects and donors have well-documented and comprehensive records and that their portfolio is accurate and up-to-date; responds to referrals of LAG prospects from the prospect researcher, development officers and student advancement ambassadors (SAA) and other sources in a timely manner. In collaboration with the prospect researcher and development leadership, works to continually evaluate, improve and implement new strategies to identify and segment LAG prospects and practices to stay current with industry practices. Fosters an environment for successful fundraising by working with development and cross-campus colleagues to gain insight on fundraising priorities and development-related engagement opportunities. Time spent: 5% Participates actively in team, divisional and campus meetings. Time spent: 5% Participates in ongoing enhancement of fundraising skills through professional training. Time spent: 5% Models behavior that demonstrates positive and respectful engagement and an approach that emphasizes action for the good of the university as a whole. Follows university and development policies and procedures. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Two or more years of fund-raising experience, preferably in higher education or equivalent experience in sales or marketing. Abilities: -Driven, self-motivated individual with proven ability to achieve financial goals and performance metrics. -Ability to forge strong working relationships with others and a capacity for engaging confidence and trust among internal and external constituents. -Outstanding oral, written and interpersonal communication skills including effective presentation skills. Ability to commuicate clearly and succinctly with diverse audiences through strong phone, email and written communication. -Demonstrable experience in developing stewardship plans leading to upgrades and retention. -Exhibits and promotes a comprehensive understanding of university-wide priorities along with the value of a UNC education. -Willingness and ability to build and maintain effective relationships with donors and colleagues while working both independently and as part of a team. -Ability to maintain a high degree of confidentiality and responsibility regarding information related to university business and confidential prospect information. -High motivation for success coupled with diplomacy, inclusivity, tact, and consistent follow-through. -Strong analytical, organizational and strategic planning skills with ability to handle multiple projects. -Problem solver who can take initiative and set priorities while being flexible. -Attention to detail and thoroughness in completing assigned duties. -Working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite and comfort working in donor/prospect computer database systems. -Ability and willingness to travel and work occasional evenings and weekends. -Must have valid driver’s license or the ability to obtain a driver’s license. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
