    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1092?c=unco": {
      "title": "Communication and Events Specialist",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Communication and Events Specialist Greeley, CO, United States | req1092 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/18/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Communication and Events Specialist COMPENSATION RANGE: 50,000.00 - 63,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Osteopathic Med Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 11/1/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 10/04/2026. Position Summary: The Communications and Event Planning Specialist supports the mission and growth of the College of Osteopathic Medicine by leading the development and execution of integrated communications and strategic events that advance the college’s identity, visibility, and engagement with key audiences. Reporting to the Associate Dean of Admissions and Student Affairs, this position is responsible for creating clear, consistent, and compelling messaging across internal and external channels, while also planning and implementing events that support recruitment, community engagement, faculty development, and institutional milestones. By aligning communications and events with the college’s strategic priorities, this role strengthens relationships with prospective students, faculty, staff, healthcare partners, and the broader community. Working within the Administrative Service Center model and in collaboration with central marketing and college leadership, the specialist manages a portfolio of projects that includes content development, digital and print communications, and end-to-end event coordination. This role ensures that all communications and events reflect the mission, values, and professional standards of osteopathic medical education while contributing to the successful launch and long-term reputation of the COM. Job Duties: Internal Communications (25-35%) - Develop and execute internal communication strategies that support faculty, staff, and student engagement within the College of Osteopathic Medicine (COM). - Develop and maintain an intranet system for the COM. - Create, write, and manage content for newsletters, announcements, websites, and other internal communication channels. - Maintain and enhance college and departmental websites, ensuring accuracy, accessibility, and alignment with institutional branding and messaging standards. - Collaborate with faculty and leadership to translate complex academic and clinical information into clear, audience-appropriate communications. - Coordinate the development of digital and print assets to support college initiatives, programs, and events. External Communications & Marketing (35-45%) - Develop, conceptualize, research, write and edit promotional and task-based information for unit publications and web content within the strategic communication guidance from University Advancement ensuring consistent university voice, tone and messaging. - Create and manage content for web, social media, and print materials to support student recruitment, faculty recruitment, and community engagement. - Develop, manage, and execute the college’s social media strategy, including content planning, posting, and audience engagement. - Collaborate with University Advancement, COM Admissions and Enrollment services, and central marketing to ensure alignment with institutional branding and recruitment goals. - Contribute to storytelling efforts that highlight student success, faculty expertise, clinical partnerships, and community impact. Event Planning & Coordination (30–35%) - Lead the planning, coordination, and execution of college events that support recruitment, engagement, and institutional visibility, including signature events such as white coat ceremonies, match day celebrations, accreditation visits, and community outreach activities. - Develop event strategies, timelines, and logistics, ensuring alignment with college priorities and compliance with university policies and procedures. - Coordinate event communications, marketing materials, and promotional efforts to maximize participation and impact. - Manage relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including faculty, staff, students, vendors, and community or clinical partners. - Oversee event logistics including scheduling, venue coordination, catering, audiovisual needs, and on-site event management. - Evaluate event effectiveness and recommend improvements based on feedback and outcomes. Other Duties as Assigned (5%) - Collaborate with the Academic Affairs and ASC communications teams to align messaging, share best practices, and support university-wide initiatives. - Contribute to the development of processes, standards, and tools that improve communication and event planning across the college. - Perform other duties as assigned in support of the College of Osteopathic Medicine. Minimum Qualifications: - Bachelor’s degree required. Substitution: Additional appropriate experience will substitute for the degree requirement on a year-for-year basis. - Four years’ work experience in a related field is required Preferred Qualifications: - Experience with website design and/or hands-on updating of website pages - Basic skill set in graphic design, marketing materials, photography, video, and/or audio productio. Substitution: Additional appropriate education will substitute for the required experience on a year-for-year basis. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1089?c=unco": {
      "title": "Graduate Thesis, Dissertation, and Graduate Faculty Specialist",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Graduate Thesis, Dissertation, and Graduate Faculty Specialist Greeley, CO, United States | req1089 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/18/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Graduate Thesis, Dissertation, and Graduate Faculty Specialist COMPENSATION RANGE: 58,000.00 - 63,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: Graduate School Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 10/15/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 10/03/2026. Position Summary: This work unit exists to ensure the quality of graduate education at UNC through administering and coordinating the graduate admissions, student records, and graduation processes; by setting and monitoring standards for graduate level admissions, student performance, graduation requirements, curricula, degree programs and membership in the graduate faculty in conjunction with the Graduate Council; and by promoting and supporting scholarship among graduate faculty and students. The purpose of this position is to serve graduate students and graduate faculty. Specifically, the position provides administrative support to the graduate faculty and doctoral research endorsement processes for graduate faculty, maintaining records of graduate faculty status, processing doctoral committee appointments and changings, scheduling doctoral exams, informing students about how to format their thesis and dissertations, and providing a thorough formatting review of students’ theses, dissertations, or doctoral scholarly projects before they are loaded into online access platforms. This position also has a major role in problem solving situations in regard to graduate students, their programs and interaction with graduate faculty during the course of student programs. Job Duties: Advise students proactively on the formatting of their thesis, scholarly project, and dissertation documents, providing workshops on formatting; teach students how to use available technology to record and cite their sources, apply formats, or build templates; and change to a pre-defense review process instead of an end-of-the-semester timeline. Keep updated on the application of newer technologies to the dissertation and thesis process. Provide pre-reviews. Review, critique, and approve editorial quality of graduate student theses and dissertation projects. Recommend to Dean for final approval. Establish and maintain relationship with library staff for dissertation/thesis tracking and placement in library. (50%) Monitor doctoral committee appointments for graduate students. Record dissertation proposal submissions and report to Graduate Degree Compliance and the Registrar’s Office. (15%) Approve and process requests to schedule doctoral exams; ensuring compliance with Graduate School policies for the committee and exam timing. Work with other staff to announce the exam. (10%) Process applications for various types of status (GL, GF, GF/DRE, GFE, EGF, and EGF/DRE) for faculty teaching graduate courses or serving on doctoral committees. Send appointment or refusal letters according to decision of Graduate Dean or Graduate Faculty Council. Support Graduate School process for mentoring training. (20%) Other duties as assigned. (5%) Minimum Qualifications: Education Bachelor’s degree; graduate degree preferred. Experience Two years of work in an educational environment in administration or technical support, or in teaching. Student worker or GA experience is acceptable. Experience writing a dissertation, thesis, journal article, or other published work is preferred. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1088?c=unco": {
      "title": "Assistant to the Dean",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Assistant to the Dean Greeley, CO, United States | req1088 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Assistant to the Dean COMPENSATION RANGE: 48,000.00 - 51,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Osteopathic Med Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 11/1/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 09/28/2026. Position Summary: This position operates with a high degree of autonomy and independent judgment to manage complex scheduling and communications across multiple senior leaders, coordinate leadership meetings and priorities, and ensure the efficient and professional operation of the Dean’s Office and shared administrative spaces. Reporting to the Dean, the Executive Assistant serves as a central point of coordination and communication for the college, acting as a key liaison to faculty, staff, students, clinical partners, and campus stakeholders, while handling sensitive matters with discretion and professionalism. In addition to executive support, this role leads facilities and front-facing operations for the college, including oversight of mail and deliveries, coordination of conference and auxiliary space scheduling, and management of front desk duties and services to ensure a welcoming, organized, and responsive environment. By integrating executive support with operational functions, this position enables informed decision-making, supports leadership effectiveness, and advances the strategic and operational priorities of the College of Osteopathic Medicine Job Duties: Dean’s Office and Executive Leadership Support and Operations 35% - Provides comprehensive administrative and operational support to the Dean’s Office - Oversees daily office operations to ensure smooth workflow, timely responses, and consistent service to students, faculty, staff, donors, and visitors - Serves as the primary point of contact for the Dean’s Office and Executive Leadership, responding to inquiries, greeting visitors, and directing matters appropriately based on an understanding of executive priorities and college operations - Ensures the Dean’s Office maintains a professional, organized, and welcoming environment - Assists Executive Leadership with assigned projects and initiatives and provides support, documentation, and recording of committee meetings as assigned - Prepares and sends personalized donor thank-you correspondence on behalf of the Dean, supporting relationship-building and stewardship efforts - Assist in planning, management and logistics of Dean-level and College-level events as requested Calendar, Communication, and Records Support 20% - Manages the Executive Leadership Team’s calendars, including scheduling appointments, prioritizing meeting requests, coordinating availability, and anticipating logistical needs in support of executive effectiveness - Organizes and manages incoming correspondence, documents, and materials for Executive Leadership; prepares correspondence, reports, and meeting materials on the behalf of Executive Leadership members - Provide record-keeping and documentation support for the Academic Leadership Council and Deans Council, ensuring accurate tracking and continuity - Maintains confidential records, procedural manuals, and compliance documentation in accordance with university policies, supporting institutional accountability Building & Operational Coordination 25% - Serves as the designated Building Coordinator for the College of Osteopathic Medicine, managing facilities coordination by initiating, tracking, and resolving work orders in collaboration with Facilities Management to ensure operational readiness, safety, and a professional environment. - Oversees mailroom operations and delivery coordination for the college, including receiving, sorting, and distributing mail and packages; coordinating with campus and external delivery services; and ensuring timely and accurate handling of sensitive or time-critical materials. - Manages conference room and auxiliary space scheduling for the college, serving as the central point of coordination for shared spaces, resolving scheduling conflicts, and ensuring spaces are appropriately prepared and equipped for meetings, events, and academic activities. - Coordinates front desk operations, including staffing, training, and scheduling of coverage, to ensure consistent, professional, and responsive service for students, faculty, staff, visitors, and external partners. - Manages and authorizes the procurement, coordination, and deployment of equipment, supplies, and materials for new and current employees, ensuring staff are fully operational and resources meet university standards. - Serves as a primary liaison with vendors, service providers, and campus partners (e.g., Facilities, IT, Shipping/Receiving) to coordinate services, resolve issues, and maintain continuity of operations. - Monitors building operations and proactively identifies and addresses facility, space, or service needs to support a functional, organized, and welcoming environment aligned with the college’s professional standards. Meetings, Travel, and Logistical Support 10% - Plans, schedules, and coordinates meetings for the Executive Leadership Team including managing meeting logistics - Coordinates travel arrangements and processes travel authorizations and reimbursements for the Executive Leadership Team ensuring compliance with university travel and procurement policies - Anticipates logistical needs related to meetings, travel, and events, providing proactive support to executive leadership - Leads logistical coordination for meetings and travel involving external collaborators, ensuring adherence to university procedures and supporting effective engagement with COM accreditors and partners Other Duties as Assigned 10% - Collaborates with Administrative Service Center (ASC) team members and college leadership to support cross-functional initiatives, ensure alignment with university processes, and improve operational efficiency. - Assists with special projects and strategic initiatives in support of the Dean’s Office and College of Osteopathic Medicine priorities, including startup activities, strategic planning, accreditation support, and new program implementation. Identifies opportunities to improve administrative, operational, and communication processes and recommends solutions to enhance efficiency and service delivery. - Provides backup support for other administrative or operational functions within the college as needed to ensure continuity of operations. - Participates in training, professional development, and university committees or working groups to remain current on policies, systems, and best practices. Minimum Qualifications: - Bachelor’s degree required plus 2-5 years executive leadership support. Substitution: Additional appropriate experience will substitute for the degree requirement on a year-for-year basis. - A minimum of three (3) years of progressively responsible administrative or executive support experience is required, preferably in a higher education, healthcare, or similarly complex organizational environment. Experience supporting senior-level leadership, managing complex calendars, coordinating meetings and events, and handling sensitive or confidential information is strongly preferred. - Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, coordinate across departments, and provide high-level customer service in a fast-paced environment is essential. - Experience with office operations, facilities coordination, front desk or customer-facing services, and basic purchasing or financial processes is preferred. Additional Requirements Interested applicants should submit the following: - Cover letter outlining interest and qualifications - Resume - Names and contact information of three professional references Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1087?c=unco": {
      "title": "Accounting Specialist",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Accounting Specialist Greeley, CO, United States | req1087 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 9/9/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Accounting Specialist COMPENSATION RANGE: 48,000.00 - 53,560.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Osteopathic Med Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 11/1/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 09/28/2026. Position Summary: The Accounting Specialist is a key support position within the College of Osteopathic Medicine (COM) and plays a critical role in ensuring the effective fiscal operations of the college. Reporting to the Business Operations Manager, this position exists to provide accurate, timely, and compliant accounting and purchasing support that enables the COM to fulfill its academic, clinical, research, and accreditation-related missions. The Accounting Specialist supports faculty, staff, and students by coordinating and processing a wide range of financial transactions, while serving as a liaison between the College of Osteopathic Medicine and central administrative offices such as General Accounting, Budget, Purchasing & Contracts, and Accounts Payable. This role is essential to maintaining sound financial stewardship in a complex environment that includes instructional programs, clinical activities, faculty practice plans, student-related expenses, grants, and strategic initiatives associated with launching and sustaining a medical school. This position is responsible for processing and monitoring transactions related to travel, procurement cards, reallocations, fund approvals, purchase orders, check/ACH requests, invoices, journal entries, and deposits, in accordance with university policies, state regulations, and medical education requirements. The Accounting Specialist must be proficient in the financial systems used by the university (such as Banner, budget and reporting tools, and document management systems) and use this knowledge to resolve issues, ensure compliance, and support financial reporting and audits. In addition to transaction processing, the Accounting Specialist contributes to the overall fiscal health of the College of Osteopathic Medicine by reviewing account activity, identifying discrepancies or trends, and providing analysis to support decision-making. The position supports the Business Operations Manager by helping assess how resources are allocated and spent, identifying opportunities for operational efficiencies, and ensuring that funds are used effectively to support students, faculty, and the long-term sustainability of the College. Job Duties: Fund/Account (FOAP) Management, Monitoring, and Reconciliation – 35% - Record, track, and analyze financial transactions using Banner, budgeting tools, spreadsheets, and other accounting systems. - Monitor, reconcile, and produce regular financial reports for College of Osteopathic Medicine funds, which may include state appropriations, clinical and programmatic funds, grants and research-related funds, faculty development funds, departmental cash funds, and Foundation-supported accounts. - Process journal entries, labor redistributions, and budget adjustments as necessary to ensure accurate accounting and proper fund management. - Processes and supports routine purchasing and procurement transactions in accordance with university policy; does not exercise final purchasing or contracting authority. - Support fiscal year-end closing activities, including reviewing and closing purchase orders, reconciling expenditures, and ensuring compliance with university deadlines. - Prepare historical and ad hoc financial reports for the Business Operations Manager, Dean, and College leadership to support budgeting, forecasting, accreditation planning, and strategic decision-making. - Maintain working knowledge of all financial systems used by the university, including those related to accounting, accounts payable, purchasing, and budgeting, in order to identify and resolve discrepancies and improve efficiency. Fund Approval and Financial Transactions – 30% - Serve as a primary fund approver for the College of Osteopathic Medicine, verifying FOAP availability, appropriateness of expenses, and compliance with university, state, and College policies. - Guide and assist faculty, staff, and student-facing programs in understanding and following fiscal policies, procedures, and timelines. - Review procurement card (p-card) transactions and supporting documentation after departmental review to ensure purchases fall within approved scope and policy. - Process and track travel-related transactions, including candidate recruitment, student activities, clinical travel, and accreditation-related travel reimbursements. - Process reimbursement requests, invoices, check/ACH requests, deposits, cash advances, petty cash, purchase orders, and electronic internal orders. - Assist with capital asset purchases, inventory tracking, and property control activities related to instructional, clinical, or research operations within the College. - Process financial transactions related to student awards, gifts, incentives, prizes, and honoraria in compliance with policy. Contract, Agreement, and Purchasing Coordination – 15% - Serve as the initial point of contact within the College of Osteopathic Medicine for contracts, MOUs, affiliation agreements, clinical agreements, vendor agreements, sales-related documents, and intergovernmental agreements. - Coordinate with Purchasing & Contracts and other central offices to ensure agreements are properly reviewed, executed, and compliant with university requirements. - Advise faculty and staff on procurement options, including determining whether purchases require competitive solicitation, are covered under existing university contracts, or may be purchased at the department level. - Assist College leadership and program staff with purchasing strategies related to instructional materials, simulation equipment, clinical resources, and professional services. Forms, Documentation, and Process Oversight – 10% - Review and manage accounting and purchasing forms to ensure completeness, accuracy, and compliance prior to submission. - Determine appropriate corrective actions for purchasing issues, including whether purchase orders should be reopened, amended, or replaced with new requisitions. - Maintain approval queues and ensure timely processing of transactions. - Help faculty and staff understand financial authority levels, approval limitations, and documentation requirements Training, Collaboration, and Special Assignments – 10% - Provide training, guidance, and updates to College faculty and staff when accounting or purchasing processes change. - Serve as a resource to new employees, including faculty, chairs, directors, and administrators, to support understanding of financial reporting, compliance, and university expectations. - Collaborate with the Business Operations Manager during chart of accounts changes, reorganization, or expansion of programs to request new funds or orgs and realign expenses and revenue. - Coordinate with central accounting teams and other college/unit accounting staff to align practices, share best practices, and support consistent application of fiscal policy. - Perform other related duties as assigned in support of the College of Osteopathic Medicine’s academic, clinical, and strategic objectives. - Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications: - A Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution is required. Preferred fields of study include Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, Public Administration, or a closely related discipline that provides a foundation in accounting principles, financial analysis, and fiscal management. Additional appropriate experience may substitute for the degree requirement on a year-for-year basis. - A minimum of two (2) years of professional accounting or financial support experience is required. Qualifying experience should include responsibility for functions such as fund or account management, budget monitoring, purchasing or procurement processes, reconciliation, transaction processing, and application of fiscal policies and procedures. - Equivalent experience may be substituted for the educational requirement. In lieu of a bachelor’s degree, a minimum of four (4) years of progressive accounting or financial management experience may be accepted. Qualifying experience should include hands‑on responsibility for accounting functions such as fund management, budget monitoring, purchasing, reconciliation, and compliance within a higher education, governmental, healthcare, or similarly regulated environment - Experience in a higher education, governmental, healthcare, or similarly regulated environment is Preferred. Additional Requirements Interested applicants should submit the following: - Cover letter outlining interest and qualifications - Resume - Names and contact information of three professional references Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
