    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1105?c=unco": {
      "title": "Chair of the Department of Primary Care",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Chair of the Department of Primary Care Greeley, CO, United States | req1105 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/21/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Chair of the Department of Primary Care COMPENSATION RANGE: 210,000.00 - 240,000.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Faculty DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Osteopathic Med Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 1/1/2027 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 11/04/2026. Position Summary: The full-time position of Chair of Primary Care (PC) will report to the Associate Dean for Clinical Education and Graduate Medical Education. This individual will be responsible for the planning, directing, and implementation of programs, policies, and procedures for the Department of Primary Care. The Chair has direct oversight and management of all faculty in the department. Additionally, the Chair will participate in curricular development and teaching of all aspects of clinical medicine throughout the curriculum. Job Duties: Teaching and Curriculum Development: • Collaborate with other chairs, faculty, and administration in curriculum development, assessment, and modification of the curriculum as a part of the college’s ongoing quality improvement and assessment program. • Incorporate simulation including the use of Standardized Participants in the instruction and assessment of medical students. • Support creation and execution of interprofessional education activities within and between COM and University departments and the community at large. • Create supporting educational materials, such as lecture PowerPoints, voice overs, video recordings, and other tools, as needed. • Direct and participate in the instruction and education of medical students while providing mentorship and motivation for learning, research, and scholarly activity. Administrative Duties: • Develop strategic plans outlining the department goals and objectives. • Develop and implement departmental policies and procedures. • Prepare annual financial and budgetary plans for the department. • Oversee the daily operations of the Primary Care department. • Lead regular department meetings to discuss planning, goal setting, progress, and issues related to the Department of Primary Care. • Recruit, mentor, and evaluate all faculty/staff in the department. • Assess needs for faculty/staff to support the goals of the Department of Primary Care. • Assist in planning and delivering ongoing faculty development activities. • Participate as an active member of the UNC COM leadership team weighing in on planning, goal setting, policy development, and issues related to the COM and University. • Ensure compliance with accreditation standards and regulatory requirements. Service: • Serve on COM committees as assigned (Typical committee load is 5; all chairs participate in Academic Leadership Council, Department Chairs Council, Faculty Assembly, Curriculum Committee, and Faculty Evaluation Promotion and Tenure Committees). • Serve as a student club advisor as time allows • Participate in faculty assembly and faculty development activities. • Participate in the COM student admission process, as needed. Minimum Qualifications: Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine (DO) degree. Current Board Certification in a primary care specialty (family medicine, general internal medicine, general pediatrics). Valid and active medical license and ability to be fully licensed in Colorado. Three (3) years of academic experience as faculty at a College of Osteopathic Medicine or School of Medicine, or as faculty member in a Graduate Medical Education program. Five (5) years or more of academic experience is preferred. Eligible for coverage by UNC COM’s malpractice insurer. Relevant experience in teaching, educational design, evaluation, scholarly activity, and academic leadership in a medical education setting. Preferred Qualifications: Personal Characteristics - Promotes personal integrity, professionalism, and growth mindset as a lifestyle - Demonstrates self-motivation in fulfillment of tasks, encouraging similar behavior in colleagues and students. - Decision making is informed via stakeholder consultation - Outcomes focused; Seeks and evaluates concrete data holistically and in a timely manner. - Exemplifies dedication in service to profession, students, colleagues, and patients Academic Skills and Perspectives - Demonstrates knowledge and skill in the delivery of medical education, including creation and analysis of NBOME/NBME style exam items. - Understands common curriculum planning and assessment metrics including Foundational Undergraduate Medical Education Core Competencies, Entrustable Professional Activities (EPAs), and the COMLEX-USA Blueprint. - Has working knowledge of accreditation processes. - Proficiency in using educational software, LMS, Microsoft, and other professional resources. Encourages interdisciplinary approaches to basic medical sciences and clinical sciences education, nurturing the team mentality and empowering collaboration - Views proven traditional learning methods through the lens of technological innovation that reconceptualizes teaching and facilitates student learning. - Approaches student success holistically, encompassing the entire lifecycle of the students, from orientation through graduation - Supports UNC’s Strategic Plan Rowing, Not Drifting 2030. Culture and Engagement - Embraces an inclusive approach to managing the academic medical enterprise and builds a culture of belonging, integrity, and transparency. - Fosters a welcoming environment that encourages individuals and groups to learn and work within and across heterogeneous communities. Leadership Style and Communication - A leadership style grounded in partnership, collegiality, and consensus building, with the ability to make and stand by decisions - Communicates in a respectful and transparent manner, as an active listener, in both spoken and written communication - Establishes and communicates a clear vision to advocate for the development of all employees. - Leads by example and supports a culture of upward mobility and opportunity for team members Additional Requirements Interested applicants should submit the following: · Cover letter outlining interest and qualifications. · Curriculum Vitae (CV). · Statement of teaching philosophy and research interests. · Names and contact information of three professional references. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
    },
    "https://unco.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1106?c=unco": {
      "title": "Assistant Technical Director Campus Commons",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) Spanish (Spain) Assistant Technical Director Campus Commons Greeley, CO, United States | req1106 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/20/2026 Back to Search POSITION TITLE: Assistant Technical Director Campus Commons COMPENSATION RANGE: 48,440.00 - 59,440.00 USD per year EMPLOYMENT CLASSIFICATION: Professional Administrative DEPARTMENT: Dean Col Perf & Vis Arts Office TARGET HIRE DATE: 12/1/2026 BENEFITS: UNC’s Career Hub Candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of application; UNC cannot provide H-1B sponsorship at this time. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by 11:59pm (MT) on 11/04/2026. Position Summary: The Campus Commons Assistant Technical Director supports the safe, efficient, and professional operation of the Campus Commons Performance Hall and other PVA performance and event spaces. The position provides hands-on technical production support in lighting, audio, video, rigging, and stage operations; supervises and trains student production staff; and serves as a technical lead for rehearsals, performances, and special events. Working as part of the PVA Production Team, the Assistant Technical Director collaborates with faculty, staff, students, guest artists, and external clients to translate production requirements into safe and practical technical solutions. The position also assists with maintenance, inspection, organization, and continuous improvement of performance spaces, theatrical systems, equipment, and production procedures. Job Duties: 50% Technical Production & Event Operations Serve as technical lead or production technician for rehearsals, performances, concerts, rentals, and special events. Operate, program, install, configure, and troubleshoot theatrical lighting, audio, video, projection, communications, and related production systems. Safely operate theatrical rigging systems, personnel lifts, stage machinery, acoustic shells, and other specialized equipment. Coordinate technical setup, changeover, strike, and restoration of performance spaces. Interpret event requirements and determine safe, efficient methods for accomplishing production needs. Troubleshoot technical problems during live events and make timely operational decisions. 20% Supervision of Student Work Crew Direct, supervise, and coordinate student production crews during calls and events. Assign work, communicate expectations, monitor performance, and provide coaching and corrective feedback. Train student employees in safe operation of production equipment and established procedures. Model professional production practices, customer service, teamwork, and workplace safety. Assist the Technical Director and Production Team in developing and maintaining training materials, SOPs, and safety procedures. 20% Venue & Equipment Maintenance Perform routine inspection, maintenance, troubleshooting, cleaning, and organization of performance spaces and production equipment. Identify equipment deficiencies and safety concerns and coordinate repair or replacement with the Technical Director. Maintain organized backstage, storage, booth, shop, and technical work areas. Assist with equipment inventory, preventative maintenance, documentation, and lifecycle planning. Support improvement projects involving theatrical infrastructure and production systems. 10% Other duties as Assigned Minimum Qualifications: Position requires a Bachelor's degree in theatre, technical production, entertainment technology, music technology, or a related field and two years of relevant professional experience; or an equivalent combination of education and professional experience. Minimum experience required: Demonstrated experience in live events or theatrical production. Working knowledge of at least two production disciplines such as audio, lighting, video/projection, rigging, or stage operations. Experience troubleshooting technical systems in a live production environment. Experience directing or coordinating the work of crews. Ability to safely work at height and operate specialized theatrical equipment following training. Strong organizational, communication, and customer-service skills. Ability to work independently, prioritize competing demands, and make sound decisions under time constraints. Ability and willingness to learn unfamiliar production systems and technologies. Preferred experience: Advanced experience in one or more theatrical production disciplines. Experience with networked audio, lighting, or video systems, including Dante and DMX/sACN/Art-Net. Experience with theatrical rigging and counterweight systems. Experience with digital audio consoles and theatrical lighting control systems. Experience supervising or training student employees. Experience in higher education, performing arts centers, presenting organizations, or similar multi-use venues. Benefits: Benefits available include health, dental, vision, life and long-term disability insurance, as well as a selection of several defined contribution retirement plans for all full-time positions. Employees at UNC receive sick leave, and other leave options may be available based on position. Full-time employees are eligible to receive tuition waiver benefits, as well as dependents and spouses of UNC Employees who are employed at 0.5 FTE or above are eligible for undergraduate dependent tuition grants of up to 50%. These tuition grants will cover in-state tuition charges. Further requirements may exist. Other benefits may be available based on position. About UNC The University of Northern Colorado is a public doctoral/research institution located in Greeley, Colorado. UNC’s commitment to education began in 1889 and continues to be a transformational force for our students. Recognized as a top university for social mobility, UNC is proud to be a Hispanic Serving Institution and to serve more than 44% of our undergraduates who are the first in their family to attend college. UNC is also committed to providing our faculty and staff with the support they need to succeed as professionals, educators, and in life. Please visit UNC’s Career Hub, to learn more about UNC’s strategic plan, school programs, and employee benefits. Go Bears! EEO Statement The University of Northern Colorado offers an educational and working environment that provides equal opportunity to all members of the UNC community. In accordance with federal and state law, UNC prohibits unlawful discrimination, including harassment, based on race, color, national origin, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, pregnancy, disability, genetic information, and/or veteran status. UNC also prohibits discrimination based on gender identity, and gender expression. Retaliation against persons who oppose a discriminatory practice, file a charge of discrimination, or testify for, assist in, or participate in an investigative proceeding relating to discrimination is prohibited. Constitutionally protected expression will not be considered discrimination or harassment under this policy. It is the responsibility of all departments, employees, and students to ensure UNC’s compliance with this policy. ADA Accommodations The University of Northern Colorado is committed to providing reasonable accommodation to individuals with disabilities. If you require reasonable accommodation in completing this application, interviewing or otherwise participating in the employee selection process, please direct your inquiries to 970-351-2718 or human.resources@unco.edu. Background Check Satisfactory completion of a background check, educational check, and authorization to work in the United States is required after a conditional offer of employment has been made. The successful candidate must submit original transcripts within one (1) month of hire. This position is contingent on funding from the Colorado State Legislature, approval by the Board of Trustees, and subject to the policies and regulations of the University of Northern Colorado. Clery Act In compliance with the Clery Act, the most recent University of Northern Colorado Annual Campus Security and Fire Safety Report is available online. A paper copy can be requested at the UNC Police Department. This report contains crime statistics and policy statements related to safety, conduct processes and Title IX investigations. Apply Now Share Save Job"
